            "result_text": "Done! I've created `/app/run.py` with the `run_tasks` function. Here's how it works:\n\n- **Concurrency control**: Uses `asyncio.Semaphore` to limit how many tasks run at once\n- **Interrupt handling**: On `KeyboardInterrupt`, it cancels all remaining tasks and waits for them to complete (with `return_exceptions=True`), allowing any cleanup code in finally blocks to run\n- **Re-raises interrupt**: After cleanup, it re-raises the `KeyboardInterrupt` so the caller knows the run was interrupted\n\nYou can use it like:\n```python\nfrom run import run_tasks\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def some_task():\n    try:\n        # your work\n        pass\n    finally:\n        # cleanup code always runs, even on Ctrl+C\n        pass\n\nasyncio.run(run_tasks([some_task, another_task], max_concurrent=5))\n```"
